<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saxons</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saxon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Saxons were a Germanic people of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, whose name was later carried by the duchy and electorate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> conquered them in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxon-wars">Saxon Wars</a>, and their region later produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. In the early modern period the Saxon Wettin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-ii-the-strong">Augustus II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a> were also kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> profiles refer to Saxons in this later context.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest possible record of the Saxons is a reference in Ptolemy's Geographia to a tribe north of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> differ, some reading Saxones and others Axones, and its interpretation is disputed. The first undoubted use of the name comes from the third to fifth centuries, when it described coastal raiders who attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> by boat from north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. Scholars believe the term was then used something like the much later word <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> and was probably not the name of any specific tribe. The raiders included Frisians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutes">Jutes</a>, as well as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a> who became established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The name has traditionally been derived from the seax, a kind of knife.</p><p>The continental Saxons appear as inland opponents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> in the sixth century, after the Franks conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>, about 531. They do not seem to have been politically united until the generations of conflict that led to their defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. Frankish rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a>, Merovingian and Carolingian, fought many campaigns against them. Charlemagne conquered them in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxon-wars">Saxon Wars</a> of 772–804 and forced them to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. He deported 10,000 Nordalbingian Saxons to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a>. In 804 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> became a Carolingian stem duchy.</p><p>The dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> expanded eastwards at the expense of the Slavic-speaking Wends. They became kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> with Henry I the Fowler in 919 and then emperors, beginning with his son Otto I, in the tenth century, but lost this position in 1024. The duchy was divided in 1180 when Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> refused to follow Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> into war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, writing about 730, remarks that the continental Saxons had no king but were governed by several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satraps</a>, who cast lots for leadership in war and were equal in peacetime. Their territory was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastphalia">Eastphalia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angria">Angria</a>, comprising about one hundred districts called Gaue. Sources on Saxon paganism come only from after their conversion and were written by Christians. The Old Saxon Baptismal Vow required converts to renounce the worship of Thunaer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woden">Wodan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxnot">Saxnot</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s capitulary prohibited cremation burial, punishable by death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Saxons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> no longer form a distinct people, but their name survives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, which includes most of the original duchy. Old Saxon evolved into Middle Low German and then Low German, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saxons” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Saxon Wars (772–804) subjected the pagan <b>Saxons</b> east of the Rhine through more than thirty years of brutal campaigning, mass deportations and forced Christianisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>As Polish power fragmented after Bolesław’s death in 1138, the dukes turned towards the German princes: after defeat by <b>Saxon</b> and Danish forces at Verchen in 1164 they submitted to Henry the Lion, and in 1181 Bogislaw I received his duchy as an imperial fief from Frederick Barbarossa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Saxons</b> of northern Germany, between the Rhine and the Elbe, were conquered by Charlemagne in a series of brutal wars between 772 and 804, during which the Saxon leader Widukind submitted and was baptised in 785.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Thirty Years’ War John George I first stayed neutral, then joined Gustavus Adolphus of Sweden; the <b>Saxon</b> contingent fled at the Battle of Breitenfeld in 1631, and Saxony suffered heavily from both sides before its separate Peace of Prague with the emperor in 1635.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Frankish pirates ravaged the coasts of Gaul and Britain; Carausius was given command of the Channel fleet against Frankish and <b>Saxon</b> raiders around 285, and a famous story tells of Frankish prisoners settled by Probus on the Black Sea who seized ships and sailed home through the Mediterranean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/franks">Franks · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1848–49 contingents were used against the Danes in Schleswig and against revolutionaries in Baden, and in 1863 <b>Saxon</b> and Hanoverian troops carried out a federal execution in Holstein.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Military</a></li></ul></section>
